Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), used only through the bound of [[lem-cm-mass-moments-are-finite]] that makes the two integrands of clause 1 $\nu$-integrable. Let $J\subseteq\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points, let $\nu$ be a **centred** mass distribution on $\mathbb R^3$ with total mass $m$ and inertia matrix $I$ ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]), let $c:J\to\mathbb R^3$ and $R:J\to SO(3)$ be differentiable, and let $x(t,\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\rho$ be the rigid placement with body angular velocity $\omega$ ([[def-cm-rigid-placement-and-body-angular-velocity]]). For $t\in J$ define
$$K(t):=\frac12\int\lVert\partial_tx(t,\rho)\rVert_2^2\,d\nu(\rho),\qquad L_c(t):=\int\bigl(x(t,\rho)-c(t)\bigr)\times\bigl(\partial_tx(t,\rho)-c'(t)\bigr)\,d\nu(\rho),$$
the second integral read componentwise ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]). Then for every $t\in J$:

1. $\partial_tx(t,\rho)=c'(t)+R(t)\bigl(\omega(t)\times\rho\bigr)$ for every $\rho\in\mathbb R^3$, and $R'(t)=R(t)[\omega(t)]_\times=[R(t)\omega(t)]_\times R(t)$; moreover the two integrands displayed above are $\nu$-integrable, so $K(t)$ and $L_c(t)$ are a well-defined finite real number and vector;
2. $K(t)=\tfrac12m\lVert c'(t)\rVert_2^2+\tfrac12\omega(t)^{\mathsf T}I\omega(t)$;
3. $L_c(t)=R(t)I\omega(t)$.

Let $F$ be either $\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$, with conjugation equal to the identity on $\mathbb R$ and with complex conjugation as in [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]. An **inner product** on an $F$-vector space $V$ is a function $\langle\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,\rangle:V\times V\to F$ such that for all $u,v,w\in V$ and $a,b\in F$:

1. $\langle au+bv,w\rangle=a\langle u,w\rangle+b\langle v,w\rangle$;
2. $\langle u,v\rangle=\overline{\langle v,u\rangle}$;
3. $\langle v,v\rangle$ is real and nonnegative, and $\langle v,v\rangle=0$ if and only if $v=0$.

The first two clauses imply conjugate-linearity in the second argument: $\langle u,av+bw\rangle=\overline a\langle u,v\rangle+\overline b\langle u,w\rangle$. This is the linear-first convention of [[def-sesquilinear-and-hermitian-forms-over-a-field-with-involution]]. A vector space equipped with an inner product is an **inner product space**.

For a positive real $p$, the following are equivalent:

1. $p=\pi$;
2. $p$ is the least positive zero of sine;
3. $p/2$ is the least positive zero of cosine;
4. $2p$ is the least positive common period of sine and cosine.
